# >>> 42 shared zshrc >>>
# Shared shell helpers for the Linux 42 campus computer.
# Edit "Useful .zshrc edits (addition)" in the shared repository, then run:
#   synczshrc
#   source ~/.zshrc
# synczshrc updates this marked block and the installed helpers, backing up .zshrc.
# First install on another computer (preserves existing content):
#   python3 "/path/to/42_Singapore_SUTD/sync_zshrc.py"
# Keep machine-specific settings OUTSIDE the markers in your local .zshrc.
# setupzshrc walks through paths, services, brightness, and automatic updates.
# ~/.42-shell-settings.zsh stores personal values and marks setup complete.
# It is loaded before shared defaults; syncing this block never replaces it.
# Run setupzshrc again to change choices, or edit that file and reload .zshrc.
# ZSHRC_SETTINGS_FILE optionally selects a different personal settings file.
# ZSHRC_SHARED_ROOT selects the update clone, separate from MAIN_REPO_ROOT.
# Helpers are installed in ~/.local/share/42-shell (ZSHRC_RUNTIME_ROOT).
# Updates refresh this local copy; deleting the update clone leaves helpers usable.
# Project repositories and optional services remain separate dependencies.
# ZDOTDIR selects the .zshrc directory (default: HOME).

# --- dailylogin: start-of-day setup and end-of-day syncing ---
# Run dailylogin explicitly; sourcing this file only loads settings and helpers.
# First dailylogin runs setup if needed, then returns without launching tasks.
# Run dailylogin again after reviewing setup to start the chosen workflow.
# With autoupdatezshrc=TRUE, first pull the shared repository, sync this block,
# and reload .zshrc. Failure in those steps stops the remaining workflow.
# Then run these tasks in order, with progress and a final result summary:
#  1. start_codex: install if missing, configure full access, request a tmux window.
#  2. syncdocs: pull other repositories under DOCUMENTS_REPOS_ROOT.
#  3. start_lan42: request the LAN42 lobby window.
#  4. start_mailbox: check/start the enrolled campus mailbox worker.
#  5. stayon start --automatic: request the separately installed Stayon runner.
#  6. adjust_brightness: set screen brightness to setbrightness (0-100).
#  7. loginbefore12 and hourslogged: report local login history.
#  8. curproj: enter the project, run make fclean, and open/reuse VS Code.
#  9. Request Chrome if it is not already running for this user.
# 10. syncproj: pull the main repository, then commit/push if there are changes.
# Finally show the tmux workspace. Repeated runs reuse running managed windows,
# but repeat reports, project cleaning, and Git tasks; this is not a once-only job.

# --- Controls ---
# TRUE/1 enables a task; FALSE/0 disables it. Boolean TRUE is case-insensitive.
# autoupdatezshrc: pull/sync/reload this configuration before running tasks.
# startcodex, startlan42, startmailbox, startstayon: enable their dailylogin tasks.
# pullotherrepos: enable syncdocs. adjustbrightness: enable adjust_brightness.
# verbosity=FALSE hides workflow reporting; individual commands still print output.
# verbosity_level=1 shows progress; 2 or higher also shows controls and settings.
# loginreports, openproject, openchrome, syncmainrepo control reports, project
# clean/open, Chrome, and main-repository Git sync respectively.
# New users opt into optional services; setup defaults automatic updates to off.
# startstayon controls dailylogin only; the stayon command can still run manually.

# --- Terminal workspace ---
# DAILY_TMUX_SESSION names the workspace (default: daily).
# DAILY_TMUX_HELPER points to the installed daily_terminal.py in ZSHRC_RUNTIME_ROOT.
# workterm: enter/return to the workspace in this terminal; create it if absent.
# workwindows: list workspace windows.
# quithidden: close ALL detached sessions on the selected tmux server and their
# jobs, keeping attached sessions. This is broader than the daily workspace.
# Managed app windows reuse a running job; rerunning a helper restarts a dead one.
# Detach to leave jobs running. Ctrl-C in a job's window stops that job.
# The workspace viewer uses GNOME Terminal or xterm when a desktop window is needed.

# --- Compile and inspect programs ---
# compile is cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror; run invokes Valgrind with leak reporting.
# Examples:
#   compile ft_isalpha.c -o output
#   compile -g ft_isalpha.c libft.a -o output
#   run ./output "Hello" 42
# -g is a compiler debug-symbol flag. Put libraries after source/object files.
# Shift+Tab is bound to reverse-menu-complete for backwards completion cycling.

# --- Project paths and Git ---
# Set current_proj to your project folder, e.g. "2_pipex".
# PROJECTS_ROOT is relative to MAIN_REPO_ROOT, e.g. "Core Curriculum/Projects".
# curproj enters MAIN_REPO_ROOT/PROJECTS_ROOT/current_proj, runs make fclean,
# then code --reuse-window .; it returns early if either command fails.
# It also displays local Git history using PROJECTS_ROOT/current_proj.
# cdmain enters MAIN_REPO_ROOT.
# syncproj runs git pull and displays status. If changes exist, it stages ALL
# changes, asks for a commit title/body, commits, and pushes. Its Git steps do
# not currently stop on every error; inspect the Git output for failures.
# syncdocs pulls repositories with configured upstreams using --ff-only; it
# never commits/pushes. It excludes MAIN_REPO_ROOT and scans nested repositories,
# including Vogsphere clones and worktrees. It pulls the mailbox clone but skips
# its contents. Failed pulls are reported while other repositories continue.
# pullzshrc pulls the update clone; synczshrc installs its block and local helpers.
# If the clone is absent, both keep the installed version. A failed pull from an
# existing clone still stops dailylogin so update errors can be resolved.

# --- Reports and optional services ---
# loginbefore12 prints local login records for days with a before-noon login.
# hourslogged estimates a rolling seven-day total and time remaining to 50 hours
# from local last output, using GNU date and gawk; it does not query Intra.
# adjust_brightness tries GNOME's power service, then xbacklight.
# MAC_REMOTE_ROOT points to the enrolled mac-remote clone. Run its setup.sh and
# get this computer approved before using start_mailbox. A held worker lock
# skips startup; otherwise the helper pulls --ff-only before bash device.sh start.
# LAN42_CLONE points to 42sg-campus-lan; start_lan42 runs its lan42.sh in tmux.
# The launcher handles its own update policy when a new lobby process starts.
# STAYON_ROOT points to the separately installed private Stayon runner.
# CODEX_INSTALL_DIR and CODEX_START_DIR override the install/start directories.
# Codex defaults to ~/Documents/42_Singapore_SUTD_hnah_private/42; codex --cd selects another project.
# codex_full_access updates global approval/sandbox defaults in CODEX_HOME/config.toml
# (default directory: ~/.codex); start_codex also passes full-access launch flags.

